WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:07.641
The Transformer introduced in the lecture replaces recurrent state propagation with attention-based interactions.

00:00:07.641 --> 00:00:14.202
Begin with a vector representation for each token and add or otherwise supply positional information.

00:00:14.202 --> 00:00:19.463
Attention without position information does not by itself distinguish word order.

00:00:19.463 --> 00:00:22.971
The original model uses sinusoidal position encodings;

00:00:22.971 --> 00:00:27.064
learned position embeddings and other schemes are alternatives.

00:00:27.064 --> 00:00:33.300
These choices are part of the architecture, not automatically provided by the attention formula.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:37.489
Follow the lecture's French-to-English translation example.

00:00:37.489 --> 00:00:43.281
The encoder processes the source sequence with self-attention and a feedforward sublayer,

00:00:43.281 --> 00:00:47.576
using residual connections and normalization around the sublayers.

00:00:47.576 --> 00:00:52.196
The decoder has causal self-attention over its available target prefix,

00:00:52.196 --> 00:00:57.077
cross-attention to the encoder outputs, and its own feedforward processing.

00:00:57.077 --> 00:01:01.046
During training, shifted known target tokens supply prefixes.

00:01:01.046 --> 00:01:06.057
Masking prevents a target position from seeing the answer at later positions,

00:01:06.057 --> 00:01:08.530
which would leak training information.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.717
A Transformer is therefore more than a query-key similarity equation.

00:01:13.717 --> 00:01:18.484
Residual connections preserve an additional route through the block;

00:01:18.484 --> 00:01:21.638
normalization controls activation statistics;

00:01:21.638 --> 00:01:27.527
the feedforward network applies a learned nonlinear transformation at each position.

00:01:27.527 --> 00:01:32.083
Original post-normalization and common pre-normalization variants

00:01:32.083 --> 00:01:34.677
arrange these components differently.

00:01:34.677 --> 00:01:40.565
Encoder-only and decoder-only families use different subsets of the original design.

00:01:40.565 --> 00:01:45.192
Computing many training positions at once does not remove the need

00:01:45.192 --> 00:01:51.080
to generate later autoregressive output tokens after their earlier sampled prefixes.

00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:55.080
Queries ask what information a position seeks.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.829
Keys describe how candidate source positions can match that request.

00:01:59.829 --> 00:02:02.692
Values supply the content to be combined.

00:02:02.692 --> 00:02:08.698
In self-attention, all three projections derive from the same sequence representation.

00:02:08.698 --> 00:02:14.355
Multiply queries by the transpose of keys to get a score for each query-key pair,

00:02:14.355 --> 00:02:17.638
and divide by the square root of key dimension.

00:02:17.638 --> 00:02:22.387
Apply softmax across the allowed keys separately for each query row.

00:02:22.737 --> 00:02:24.820
Work through one row.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.605
The query is 1, 0.

00:02:26.605 --> 00:02:29.680
The two keys are 1, 0 and 0, 1.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.755
Their dot products are 1 and 0.

00:02:32.755 --> 00:02:39.699
Key dimension is 2, so scaled scores are approximately 0.707107 and 0.

00:02:39.699 --> 00:02:44.658
Softmax gives approximately 0.669762 and 0.330238.

00:02:44.658 --> 00:02:51.998
For numerical stability, subtract the largest score before exponentiating;

00:02:51.998 --> 00:02:57.057
this leaves the normalized probabilities unchanged.

00:02:57.407 --> 00:02:59.538
Take scalar values 2 and 10.

00:02:59.538 --> 00:03:02.964
Their weighted sum is approximately 4.641908.

00:03:02.964 --> 00:03:05.628
Calculations use unrounded weights.

00:03:05.628 --> 00:03:11.261
This is a mixture, not a hard retrieval of only the highest-scoring value.

00:03:11.261 --> 00:03:16.438
For a sequence with n query positions, the attention map has n rows;

00:03:16.438 --> 00:03:18.341
each valid row sums to 1.

00:03:18.341 --> 00:03:24.507
Key and value dimensions can differ, provided the matrix products are compatible.

00:03:24.507 --> 00:03:29.227
The page lets you modify the query and inspect scores, weights

00:03:29.227 --> 00:03:31.967
and the resulting output separately.

00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:35.894
Causal self-attention must exclude future keys.

00:03:35.894 --> 00:03:41.614
Add a mask that assigns negative infinity to forbidden scores before applying softmax.

00:03:41.614 --> 00:03:43.477
Their exponentials become 0.

00:03:43.477 --> 00:03:48.200
With our two-value example, if the query belongs to the first position,

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.196
only the first key is allowed.

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:52.923
Its weight becomes 1 and the output is 2.

00:03:52.923 --> 00:03:55.850
Each row must retain at least one valid key;

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:58.710
masking every key requires special handling

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:03.367
rather than treating undefined normalization as a useful distribution.

00:04:03.717 --> 00:04:08.930
With two equal finite allowed scores, softmax gives 0.5 to each key,

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.460
and values 2 and 10 average to 6.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:17.362
Padding masks separately exclude positions introduced only to batch sequences

00:04:17.362 --> 00:04:18.819
of unequal lengths.

00:04:18.819 --> 00:04:24.722
Do not normalize first and then zero forbidden weights without renormalizing:

00:04:24.722 --> 00:04:28.785
the remaining row would generally no longer sum to 1.

00:04:28.785 --> 00:04:33.614
Also distinguish masking from assigning a low but finite score.

00:04:33.614 --> 00:04:39.517
The latter can leave a small, nonzero contribution from a forbidden position.

00:04:39.867 --> 00:04:43.789
Cross-attention uses queries from one representation sequence

00:04:43.789 --> 00:04:48.997
and keys and values from another, such as decoder states querying encoder states.

00:04:48.997 --> 00:04:53.177
The score map can be rectangular: target length by source length.

00:04:53.177 --> 00:04:56.713
The lecture's alignment and pronoun examples illustrate

00:04:56.713 --> 00:04:59.092
context-dependent information mixing,

00:04:59.092 --> 00:05:03.593
not proof that attention weights are a complete linguistic explanation

00:05:03.593 --> 00:05:05.137
or a causal attribution.

00:05:05.137 --> 00:05:10.602
To debug an implementation, verify query and key axes, masking direction and row sums

00:05:10.602 --> 00:05:14.267
before examining whether a visualization looks intuitive.

00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:20.861
Multi-head attention learns separate query, key and value projections for several heads.

00:05:20.861 --> 00:05:23.759
Each head computes its own attention output.

00:05:23.759 --> 00:05:29.290
Concatenate the head outputs and apply an output projection back to the model width.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:32.780
Different heads can represent different interactions,

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:37.718
but they are not guaranteed to correspond to simple named linguistic roles.

00:05:37.718 --> 00:05:43.249
The positionwise feedforward network then transforms each position's feature vector,

00:05:43.249 --> 00:05:47.990
usually expanding and contracting its internal width with a nonlinearity

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:49.307
between projections.

00:05:49.657 --> 00:05:55.748
For a concrete shape example, use 3 token positions, model width 4 and 2 heads,

00:05:55.748 --> 00:05:58.215
each with key and value width 2.

00:05:58.215 --> 00:06:02.378
Each head has a 3x3 score map and a 3x2 value mixture.

00:06:02.378 --> 00:06:09.163
Concatenation produces a 3x4 representation, followed by a compatible output projection.

00:06:09.163 --> 00:06:14.020
Adding a residual requires matching the original feature width.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:19.340
This small example makes clear that a head count is not a token count

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:24.737
and the attention map's axes are positions, not embedding coordinates.

00:06:25.087 --> 00:06:30.388
Compared with recurrent state updates, attention provides direct interactions

00:06:30.388 --> 00:06:32.660
between distant allowed positions

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:36.928
and easier parallel computation over known training positions.

00:06:36.928 --> 00:06:41.059
Standard full attention also constructs a quadratic-size map

00:06:41.059 --> 00:06:44.225
and uses quadratic sequence-length score work.

00:06:44.225 --> 00:06:48.907
Autoregressive inference remains sequential across generated tokens,

00:06:48.907 --> 00:06:53.726
although cached keys and values can avoid recomputing old projections.

00:06:53.726 --> 00:06:57.650
The lecture's model-family catalog and market-style chart

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:01.987
are historical illustrations, not current limits or guarantees.

00:07:01.987 --> 00:07:04.809
Applications include language and vision,

00:07:04.809 --> 00:07:08.527
with architecture and evaluation tailored to the data.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.094
Pause for the final check.

00:07:11.094 --> 00:07:20.405
In a correctly masked causal attention layer, can changing the value at a future position affect the first position's attention output?

00:07:20.405 --> 00:07:24.406
What property of the weight matrix guarantees your answer?

00:07:24.406 --> 00:07:32.614
Then explain how the answer changes for an encoder's unrestricted self-attention or a decoder's source cross-attention.

00:07:32.614 --> 00:07:41.580
Do not describe all three simply as looking at the sentence; specify the origin of the query vectors and key vectors in each case.

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:46.811
A correctly masked causal first row gives future keys 0 weight,

00:07:46.811 --> 00:07:50.823
so changing a future value cannot affect that mixture.

00:07:50.823 --> 00:07:55.430
An encoder can normally combine all unpadded source positions.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:08:01.522
Cross-attention normally lets decoder queries access the available encoder source,

00:08:01.522 --> 00:08:03.528
subject to its source mask.

00:08:03.528 --> 00:08:08.134
Your exercise is to implement one small attention calculation,

00:08:08.134 --> 00:08:11.255
assert the row sums and forbidden weights,

00:08:11.255 --> 00:08:16.010
and then deliberately reverse a mask to see how a numerical test

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:18.610
detects future-information leakage.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.930
The 3 lectures now form a continuous path.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:27.092
Feedforward networks learn representations and use graph-based gradients.

00:08:27.092 --> 00:08:31.194
Convolution shares local filters across spatial positions.

00:08:31.194 --> 00:08:36.285
Recurrent networks share state updates across time, gates manage memory,

00:08:36.285 --> 00:08:41.730
and Transformers combine attention with positional and feedforward structure.

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:45.973
Use the complete notes, executable labs and worked exercises

00:08:45.973 --> 00:08:48.377
to reconstruct these computations.

00:08:48.377 --> 00:08:54.105
Architectural names are useful only when you can explain the values, dependencies

00:08:54.105 --> 00:08:58.560
and training objective that the architecture actually computes.

